{"verdict":"approved","issues":[{"severity":"major","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":23,"description":"SPEC: The plan of record prescribes importing `Stack`, `getSingleEntry`, and `getSingletonEntry` from `src/services/contentstack.ts` into `_app.tsx`, which is a universal (client + server) module. That service module reads `process.env` at import time (API_KEY, DELIVERY_TOKEN, CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN) and contains a validation path that throws `Missing required environment variables`. In the browser bundle, non-`NEXT_PUBLIC_` env vars are undefined, so (a) `live_preview.enable` evaluates to `Boolean('')` === false client-side, meaning the delivery SDK will not route re-fetches through the preview host or apply the live-preview hash — the re-fetch may return published rather than draft content; and (b) if the module-level env validation executes on import in the client bundle, every page crashes. If the project instead inlines these vars into the client bundle, the delivery token and preview/management token ship to browsers. This is a gap in the PLAN (it prescribed `stackSdk: Stack` in `_app.tsx`), not an implementer deviation — the implementer followed the plan of record. It needs verification in a real preview session before this ships.","suggestedFix":"Verify in a running preview session that the client-side re-fetch actually returns draft entries. If not, either expose a client-safe preview path (e.g. a Next.js API route that performs the getSingleEntry/getSingletonEntry re-fetch server-side and is called from the onEntryChange callback), or gate the env-dependent module so the client bundle never evaluates the server-only validation. Update the plan of record accordingly."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/404.tsx","line":26,"description":"404.tsx is not named in the plan of record and the 404 page is not part of the acceptance criteria (MX Homepage and Discover Article only). The added subscription (~10 lines) is harmless and consistent with the [[...slug]].tsx pattern, but it is scope beyond the prescribed fix.","suggestedFix":"Acceptable to keep for consistency, but note it in the PR description; alternatively drop it to keep the changeset to plan-named files only."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/[[...slug]].tsx","line":37,"description":"`useState(initialContent)` captures only the first props value; if Next.js ever re-renders this component with new `content` props without remounting (e.g. shallow client-side navigation between slugs resolved to the same page component), the state would be stale. Next typically remounts page components on route change, so this is low-risk, and the pre-existing code read props directly.","suggestedFix":"Optional: add `useEffect(() => setContent(initialContent), [initialContent])` or key the component by route if stale-content-on-navigation is observed."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/services/contentstack.ts","line":77,"description":"The plan prescribed `preview_token` in the live_preview block, but the installed contentstack SDK (3.17.2) declares and consumes `management_token` (verified in node_modules/contentstack/index.d.ts line 123 and the dist bundle). The implementer used the correct, actually-consumed key — this note is to record that the plan's wording was wrong, not the code. `enable: Boolean(CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN)` is also a sensible deviation from `enable: true` (prevents the SDK routing to the preview host when no token is configured).","suggestedFix":"No change to code; correct the plan of record to say `management_token`."}],"summary":"The implementation follows the plan of record: the live_preview block is added to the Stack options in src/services/contentstack.ts using keys the installed SDK (3.17.2) actually consumes (verified: enable/host/management_token are all read by the runtime and declared in index.d.ts), CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN is added to the env destructuring, and the API host default uses the new CONTENTSTACK_DEFAULT_API_HOST named constant rather than a hardcoded string. _app.tsx initializes @contentstack/live-preview-utils (declared in package.json and installed) with stackSdk and an onEntryChange callback that reuses the existing getSingleEntry/getSingletonEntry helpers — no hand-rolled fetch logic. The context change deviates from the plan's literal prescription (a module-level pub-sub plus per-page subscription in [[...slug]].tsx instead of an updater field on IContentstackContext driven from _app.tsx), but this is a reasonable adaptation: the plan's premise that ContentstackContext.Provider lives in _app.tsx is factually wrong for this codebase — the Provider is in pages/[[...slug]].tsx — so the implementer put the reactivity where the Provider actually is. The deviation is not materially larger than the prescription. The shipped spec file covers all six verification criteria with clearly named it() blocks (homepage draft swap, discover-article draft swap, no-signal no-regression, unsubscribe isolation, successive draft changes in sequence, and gated/throwing subscriber isolation). No pasted credentials — all token values are process.env references. TypeScript casts (Stack as unknown as IStackSdk, updatedPage as I404) carry explanatory comments. One significant advisory (SPEC, major): the plan-prescribed client-side import of the server Stack module means live_preview.enable is false in the browser bundle (non-NEXT_PUBLIC_ env vars are undefined client-side), so the preview re-fetch may return published content — this is a plan gap to verify in a live preview session, not an implementer failure. Verdict: approved, with the SPEC advisory flagged for the plan owner."}
